<TopicPage slug="subjects/aerospace-engineering/foundational/aerodynamics-i-lift-and-airfoils">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Flight is a momentum trade. Every second it flies, an airliner's wings seize several tonnes
    of passing air, bend the airflow downward, and — by Newton's third law — receive an equal
    and opposite push upward. That push is lift, and it is not a mystery: it can be measured,
    predicted to a fraction of a percent in a wind tunnel, and computed from one master
    equation. Aerodynamics is the discipline of managing that trade — of shaping surfaces so
    that the air, doing nothing more exotic than obeying Newton's laws and conserving its own
    mass and energy, cooperates with the machine.
  </p>
  <p>
    What surprises newcomers is how much of the subject rests on just two conservation
    statements. Conserve mass in a flowing stream and you get <em>continuity</em>: narrow the
    channel and the flow must speed up. Conserve energy along that stream and you get
    <em>Bernoulli's principle</em>: where the flow speeds up, its pressure drops. Those two
    facts, applied to the curved surface of a wing, explain why the pressure above an airfoil
    falls and why the wing — sitting between low pressure above and near-ambient pressure
    below — is pushed upward. The famous lift equation is simply these conservation laws plus
    one measured number, the lift coefficient, that captures how good a particular shape is at
    the trick.
  </p>
  <p>
    This chapter builds that chain in order: what a fluid is and how it flows, how speed and
    pressure trade against each other, how a wing exploits the trade, and finally how real
    wings differ from ideal ones — stall, three-dimensionality and the drag that lift always
    owes.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="how a wing makes lift" widget="equationStep" config={liftStepsCfg} />

  <h2>Fluid basics and continuity</h2>
  <p>
    Air behaves, for the speeds in this chapter, as an incompressible fluid: its density is
    essentially fixed (1.225 kg per cubic metre at sea level on a standard day), and it flows
    in smooth layers we trace as <em>streamlines</em>. The single most useful statement about
    such a flow is continuity: mass cannot pile up anywhere, so the volume of air passing any
    cross-section per second is the same everywhere. Write it as A × V = constant — cross-
    sectional area times speed — and it becomes immediately tangible.
  </p>
  <p>
    Squeeze a garden hose with your thumb and the jet quickens; the same water flow per second
    now exits through a smaller hole, so its speed must rise. A wind tunnel works the same way
    by design: a duct of 0.1 square metre cross-section fed at 10 m per second carries
    1 cubic metre — 1.225 kg — of air each second. Narrow that duct to 0.05 square metres and
    continuity demands the speed double to 20 m per second. Nothing "pushes" the air faster in
    a mysterious way; a traffic-jam argument does it: if the front of the queue sped up, a gap
    would open, and the pressure gradient ahead smooths the flow until the bookkeeping closes.
  </p>

  <h2>Bernoulli: the speed–pressure trade</h2>
  <p>
    Where continuity conserves mass, Bernoulli's principle conserves energy along a
    streamline. For steady flow of an ideal fluid: pressure + ½ρV² = constant. The term
    ½ρV² — half the density times the speed squared — is the <em>dynamic pressure</em>, the
    kinetic energy of a cubic metre of flow, and it is the currency the trade is made in.
    Speed up the flow and the static pressure must fall to pay for it; slow it down and the
    pressure recovers.
  </p>
  <p>
    The numbers are modest but real. At 80 m per second, the dynamic pressure is
    ½ × 1.225 × 80² ≈ 3,920 pascals — about 4 percent of atmospheric pressure. Our narrowing
    duct, accelerating the flow from 10 to 20 m per second, drops its static pressure by
    ½ × 1.225 × (20² − 10²) ≈ 184 pascals. This is exactly how a venturi measures airflow,
    how a carburettor once drew fuel into an airstream, and how a perfume atomiser lifts
    liquid into a jet. Hold a strip of paper below your lips, blow across the top, and the
    paper rises: you have run Bernoulli's experiment on a budget, with the fast flow above the
    strip at lower pressure than the still air below it.
  </p>

  <h2>Lift and circulation: the wing at work</h2>
  <p>
    Now tilt and curve that accounting into a wing. An airfoil is shaped so that the flow
    leaving its trailing edge is deflected downward; the streamlines above are squeezed
    together (faster flow, by continuity) and the pressure there falls (by Bernoulli). Most of
    lift — roughly two-thirds for a typical section — comes from this suction on the upper
    surface rather than pushing from below. Measured over the whole airfoil, the pressure
    difference integrates to a force, and that force points up and slightly backward: lift
    and induced drag.
  </p>
  <p>
    The formal description is <em>circulation</em>: the wing leaves the air with a net
    rotating motion superimposed on the stream, and the Kutta–Joukowski theorem states that
    lift per unit span equals density × speed × circulation. This is not a different theory
    from the momentum view — it is the same physics written in the language mathematicians
    can compute with. It also survives the sharpest test available: an aircraft flying level
    throws air downward continuously, and on a hot, humid day you can watch the result — the
    wingtip vortices spiralling behind a landing airliner are the downward momentum, made
    visible, that the lift is made of.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> air "must arrive in step" — that two particles splitting at
    the leading edge, one over the top and one under the bottom, reunite at the trailing edge,
    so the upper one travels farther and hence faster. Nothing enforces such a rendezvous, and
    measurements show the upper particle arrives far earlier. The upper flow is genuinely
    faster because the shape and angle of attack impose it, and because circulation requires
    it — not because of a scheduled meeting. The equal-transit-time story predicts no lift at
    all for a flat plate at an angle, which demonstrably flies.
  </p>

  <h2>The lift equation as a design space</h2>
  <p>
    Everything above compresses into one line: L = ½ρV²S·C<sub>L</sub>, where ρ is air
    density, V airspeed, S wing area and C<sub>L</sub> the lift coefficient — the dimensionless
    "goodness" of the wing at its current angle of attack. Each term is a design lever, and
    different aircraft pull different ones. Gliders, flying slowly, get long wings with large
    area; fighters, flying fast, need only small ones, since the speed-squared term supplies
    the lift. Try the trade with the widget: at 80 m per second with a 30 square metre wing at
    C<sub>L</sub> = 0.8 near sea level, the lift is about 94,000 newtons — enough to hold up
    roughly 9.6 tonnes. An airliner at cruise, heavier by far, buys its lift with more speed
    at altitude, larger area, or a higher coefficient — and typically cruises at a
    C<sub>L</sub> near 0.5, safely below stall.
  </p>
  <p>
    Density is the quiet term, and the widget lets you feel it. Airliners cruise near 11,000
    metres, where the air has thinned to roughly 0.4 kg per cubic metre — a third of its sea-
    level value — for a simple economic reason: drag falls with the same ½ρV² that lift does,
    so thin air is cheap air. But the lift must still equal the weight, so the aircraft
    compensates by flying faster — and it must, because at too low a speed the required
    C<sub>L</sub> would exceed the stall ceiling. This tension between thin-air efficiency and
    stall margin is why "coffin corner" exists: at very high altitude, the speed that stalls
    the wing and the speed that overspeeds it converge to within a few knots.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="drag the terms — find the wing that lifts the aircraft" widget="sliderLab" config={liftCfg} />

  <h2>Airfoil and wing characteristics, and stall</h2>
  <p>
    A section's geometry is described by its chord (length front to back) and camber (the
    curvature of its mean line). Camber sets the "built-in" lift at zero angle; angle of
    attack fine-tunes it in flight. Plot the lift coefficient against angle of attack and you
    get the airfoil's signature: a straight line rising at about 0.1 per degree through the
    normal range, then a rounded peak — C<sub>L,max</sub> typically between 1.2 and 1.6 for a
    plain wing — at around fifteen degrees. Past that critical angle the boundary layer can no
    longer cling to the suction surface; it separates, the low-pressure region collapses, and
    the wing <em>stalls</em>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Stall deserves precision, because it is misunderstood even by many passengers. It is not
    an engine problem and not caused by flying "too slowly" as such — it is caused by too much
    angle of attack, which is why it can happen at any speed if the pilot pulls hard enough,
    and why the recovery is to lower the nose and restore the flow. Wings are fitted with
    slats and flaps precisely to manage this: deployed for takeoff and landing, they increase
    camber and re-energise the boundary layer, raising C<sub>L,max</sub> to over 3 and letting
    a 70-tonne aircraft fly a stable approach at 250 km/h rather than 400.
  </p>
  <p>
    Three-dimensionality then taxes everything. A real wing leaks: high pressure beneath curls
    around the tips toward the low pressure above, forming tip vortices that tilt the lift
    vector backward. The longer and thinner the wing (higher <em>aspect ratio</em>), the
    smaller the leakage — the deep logic behind glider wings and airliner winglets. This
    <em>induced drag</em> is the price of generating lift at finite span, and it grows as
    speed drops: slow flight is the expensive regime, which is why climb-outs are flown at
    carefully chosen best rates.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Two hidden layers make the idealised picture quantitative. The first is the
    <em>boundary layer</em>: air sticks to the surface (no slip), and within a layer a few
    millimetres thick the speed climbs from zero to the full stream value. Everything
    aerodynamic — separation, stall, skin friction — is boundary-layer behaviour. Prandtl's
    1904 insight that this thin sheet governs everything is the founding act of modern
    aerodynamics. Whether that layer is laminar (smooth, low friction, fragile) or turbulent
    (chaotic, higher friction, stubbornly attached) decides both drag and stall character, and
    the switch is set by the Reynolds number — for an airliner wing chord, in the millions.
  </p>
  <p>
    The second layer is the <em>Kutta condition</em>, the quiet rule that makes circulation
    theory predictive: nature chooses precisely that amount of circulation which leaves the
    flow leaving the sharp trailing edge smoothly, rather than whipping around it at infinite
    speed. Add that condition to ideal-flow mathematics and lift comes out in closed form —
    theory and wind tunnel agreeing to a few percent for attached flow. Push the speed higher
    and compressibility enters: near Mach 0.7–0.8, air rushing over the curved upper surface
    locally exceeds the speed of sound, shock waves form, drag spikes, and the classic
    response is the swept wing, which quietly reduces the component of flow the airfoil
    actually feels. Beyond that lie the open problems: controlling boundary layers to keep
    large regions laminar (worth double-digit percentages in fuel burn), turbulence
    simulation still hostage to closure models, and the design of quiet, efficient
    high-lift systems for the next generation of aircraft configurations.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> ABET aerospace program criteria; Anderson, <em>Fundamentals of Aerodynamics</em>; FAA <em>Pilot's Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
